x86_64-linux  there is nothing to compare here, by construction: the case includes <new>, and on the cross targets clang has no C++ headers at all and refuses the file outright. What the case is about - which destructor an explicit `p->~T()` reaches, and that each object is destroyed exactly once - is a run-time question the ledger answers, and run.sh asks it on every machine.
x86_64-windows  there is nothing to compare here, by construction: the case includes <new>, and on the cross targets clang has no C++ headers at all and refuses the file outright. What the case is about - which destructor an explicit `p->~T()` reaches, and that each object is destroyed exactly once - is a run-time question the ledger answers, and run.sh asks it on every machine.
arm64-darwin  a difference about emission and not about names: cxx1 calls the placement `operator new(size_t, void *)` and its delete as the inline functions this tree's <new> defines them as (_ZnwmPv, _ZdlPvS_), emits TB's implicit inline constructor, and calls the ledger's abort, where clang inlines all four away and emits no symbol; and clang has an __Unwind_Resume for a cleanup pad round a scope cxx1 sees nothing to unwind in. Every name both compilers emit agrees, and the destructor calls the case is about are checked by run.sh.
